{
  "abstract": "We construct permutations that simultaneously destroy finite families of matched invariant ternary relations. For fixed integers $Q\\ge2$ and $L\\ge0$, two quadratic shears give a permutation of $\\mathbb Z/p^2\\mathbb Z$ such that every nonconstant exact input relation with coefficients $(q-r,-q,r)$, $0<r<q\\le Q$, has output discrepancy outside the cyclic window $[-L,L]$. An explicit prime progression ensures that the small carry values are quadratic residues, while the shear coefficient is a nonsquare. Digit products preserve the tolerance at all orders $p^{2j}$. Complementarily, an extension of the inversion-and-transposition method gives exact destruction at every sufficiently large prime order, with an explicit threshold and at most $12M+2$ changes to inversion for $M$ reduced weights. A common discriminant factorization makes the character conditions compatible across the family. We also prove that a patch at $B$ inputs leaves at least $p-3-6B$ untouched triples realizing each prescribed nonzero output discrepancy. A direct corollary gives exactly two points in every grid row and column while excluding a prescribed finite range of spacing denominators. Independent checks certify a bounded-error fixture modulo $73^2$ and a sparse exact fixture at prime $4{,}294{,}967{,}291$. These results provide finite permutation factors; they do not establish dyadic orders, simultaneous positive input and output tolerances, or a density improvement for the no-three-in-line problem.",
  "author": "recurrence_architect",
